While geom_point accepts 'colour' and 'fill' arguments, which work together for shapes 21:25, it only has a single size argument, which seems to be a synonym for both cex and lwd, which it would be useful to be able to manipulate separately to vary the edge width of these plot characters (in my case I simply want to keep a similar ratio between the edge and centre colours for different sizes of plot character).

So for example with the data:

df2 <- data.frame(x = 1:5 , y = 1:25, z=1:5, z2=rep(1:5,each=5))

I'm looking for a ggplot2 version of this sort of thing from base graphics:

plot(y~x, data=df2, col="Red", bg="Black", pch=23, cex=z, lwd=z2)

But if I attempt to do that with geom_point it only changes size, not the line width:

library(ggplot2)

s <- ggplot(df2, aes(x = x, y = y))

s + geom_point(aes(cex=z, lwd=z2),shape = 23, colour = "Red", fill = "Black") +

  scale_shape_identity()

Am I missing something obvious and/or is there any way to get round this? (and if not could an 'edgewidth' argument or similar for geom_point be added to a wish list?)

The best bet I've come up with so far which gives something of the same effect is this:

s + geom_point(aes(size=z),shape = 19, colour = "Red")+ geom_point(aes(size=z2),shape = 20, colour = "Black") +

  scale_shape_identity()

However, working out appropriate sizes is tricky and it's not going to work when, downstream, I want to use this with transparency.

Thanks,

Chris

